      <h2 id="configure-additional-devices-manually_post-install-configure-additional-devices-ibm-z" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring additional devices manually<a href="#configure-additional-devices-manually_post-install-configure-additional-devices-ibm-z" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">After installation, you can manually configure additional devices on IBM Z(R) or IBM(R) LinuxONE nodes. This configuration persists across node restarts, but you must redo the steps if you replace the node.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster as a user with administrative privileges.</li>
      <li>The device must be available to the node.</li>
      <li>In a z/VM environment, the device must be attached to the z/VM guest.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Connect to the node via SSH by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ssh <user>@<node_ip_address>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>You can also start a debug session to the node by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc debug node/<node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To enable the devices with the <code>chzdev</code> command, enter the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo chzdev -e <device>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <section class="related-resources _additional-resources">
      <p class="related-resources-title">Additional resources</p>
      <ul>
      <li><a href="https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/linux-on-systems?topic=commands-chzdev">chzdev - Configure IBM Z(R) devices (IBM(R) Documentation)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/linux-on-systems?topic=linuxonibm/com.ibm.linux.z.ludd/ludd_c_perscfg.html">Persistent device configuration (IBM(R) Documentation)</a></li>
      </ul>
      </section>
      <h2 id="ibm-z-roce-network-cards_post-install-configure-additional-devices-ibm-z" class="group scroll-mt-20">RoCE network cards<a href="#ibm-z-roce-network-cards_post-install-configure-additional-devices-ibm-z" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can configure RoCE (RDMA over Converged Ethernet) interfaces with the Kubernetes NMState Operator when RoCE network cards are available on a node. For example, the cards are available if they are attached in a z/VM environment or passed through in a RHEL KVM environment.</p>
      <h2 id="enabling-multipathing-fcp-luns_post-install-configure-additional-devices-ibm-z" class="group scroll-mt-20">Enabling multipathing for FCP LUNs<a href="#enabling-multipathing-fcp-luns_post-install-configure-additional-devices-ibm-z" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">After installation, you can enable multipathing for Fibre Channel Protocol (FCP) logical unit numbers (LUNs) on IBM Z(R) or IBM(R) LinuxONE nodes. This configuration persists across node restarts, but you must redo the steps if you replace the node.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>On IBM Z(R) and IBM(R) LinuxONE, you can enable multipathing only if you configured your cluster for it during installation. For more information, see "Installing RHCOS and starting the OpenShift Container Platform bootstrap process" in <em>Installing a cluster with z/VM on IBM Z(R) and IBM(R) LinuxONE</em>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster as a user with administrative privileges.</li>
      <li>You have configured multiple paths to a LUN with either method explained above.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Connect to the node via SSH by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ssh <user>@<node_ip_address>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>You can also start a debug session to the node by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc debug node/<node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To enable multipathing, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo /sbin/mpathconf --enable"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To start the <code>multipathd</code> daemon, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo multipath"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: To format your multipath device with fdisk, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo fdisk /dev/mapper/mpatha"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To verify that the devices have been grouped, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo multipath -ll"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"mpatha (20017380030290197) dm-1 IBM,2810XIV\n   size=512G features='1 queue_if_no_path' hwhandler='1 alua' wp=rw\n\t-+- policy='service-time 0' prio=50 status=enabled\n \t|- 1:0:0:6  sde 68:16  active ready running\n \t|- 1:0:1:6  sdf 69:24  active ready running\n \t|- 0:0:0:6  sdg  8:80  active ready running\n \t`- 0:0:1:6  sdh 66:48  active ready running"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ul>
